WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:05.184
What game can be played on the computer, blindfolded, by mail, and in a room?

2
00:00:05.185 --> 00:00:08.851
What game has a 1,500-year-old history?

3
00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:15.511
What game has been played by kings, philosophers, monks, and everyday people in countries all over the world?

4
00:00:15.512 --> 00:00:21.175
The answer is chess━a game, a sport, and a mental workout all in one!

5
00:00:21.176 --> 00:00:27.571
Chess is a board game played between 2 people on a square board of 64 black and white squares.

6
00:00:27.572 --> 00:00:34.943
Each player begins the game with 16 pieces, and each piece can be moved and used to capture other pieces on the board.

7
00:00:34.944 --> 00:00:44.262
The pieces are called pawns (8 per player), knights (2), bishops (2), rooks (2), queens (1), and kings (1).

8
00:00:44.263 --> 00:00:50.094
One player controls all the white pieces; the other player controls all the black pieces.

9
00:00:50.095 --> 00:00:53.695
The object of the game is to capture your opponent’s king.

10
00:00:53.696 --> 00:00:55.687
The History of Chess

11
00:00:55.688 --> 00:01:04.199
Chess is reputed to be the world’s most popular game and is currently played recreationally and competitively by millions around the world.

12
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:11.141
The origins of chess are contentious, but it seems that chess was invented in India in the sixth century AD.

13
00:01:11.142 --> 00:01:25.410
The Sanskrit word for chess, chaturanga , means "four parts" because chess was modeled on an Indian army platoon, which had four parts─elephants, chariots, soldiers on horseback, and foot soldiers─as well as a

14
00:01:25.411 --> 00:01:35.832
king. In ancient India, chess was a gambling game played with four players and dice, but when the game spread to Persia, modern-day Iran, it quickly developed

15
00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:47.945
further. The Persians translated the old Indian names into Persian, reduced the number of players to two, discarded the dice, and added a new piece, an advisor, which would later become the

16
00:01:47.946 --> 00:01:51.707
queen. Chess became a game of strategy and

17
00:01:51.708 --> 00:02:00.593
intelligence. The most important word in a game of chess is "checkmate"; it means that a player’s king can’t escape capture, so the game is over.

18
00:02:00.594 --> 00:02:05.982
Interestingly, this word comes from the Persian word shah-mat , or

19
00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:12.741
"king-help." The Persians didn’t have time to make any more changes to chess because the Arabs invaded in 614

20
00:02:12.742 --> 00:02:19.789
AD. In Persia, the Arabs took up chess and were very serious players, with chess professionals employed at

21
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:24.630
court. In the seventh century, the Arabs conquered Spain and chess came to

22
00:02:24.631 --> 00:02:32.851
Europe. Chess became popular throughout the continent, but there was great confusion concerning the names of the pieces, and again the game

23
00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:39.557
changed. The elephant piece became an archer in Spain, a messenger in Germany, and a court jester in

24
00:02:39.558 --> 00:02:48.278
France. In 1580, an Italian made the radical suggestion that the queen become the strongest piece in the game, rather than the

25
00:02:48.279 --> 00:02:51.175
weakest. The game underwent major changes

26
00:02:51.176 --> 00:02:57.702
again. The names of the pieces were standardized, and the average chess game was now played in half the time it used to take to

27
00:02:57.703 --> 00:03:04.146
play. This new game was called Crazy Chess by the Italians and Chess of the Angry Queen by the

28
00:03:04.147 --> 00:03:10.923
French. Eventually, it became the norm, and the rules of this version are the rules that modern chess players use

29
00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:16.542
today. Until the twentieth century, chess was only played by the wealthy and highly

30
00:03:16.543 --> 00:03:22.371
educated. Today, it is played in parks, clubs, libraries, and homes by people of all

31
00:03:22.372 --> 00:03:27.918
classes. In Russia, chess is considered a national sport and is more popular than

32
00:03:27.919 --> 00:03:33.399
soccer. In fact, Russian chess players have been the best in the world since the

33
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.510
1940s. The Computer

34
00:03:35.511 --> 00:03:41.834
Revolution The twentieth century has also seen a revolution in chess with the advent of computer

35
00:03:41.835 --> 00:03:52.519
technology. In 1985, a Chinese doctoral student named Feng-hsiung Hsu, who was studying in the U.S., began to construct a chess-playing computer called

36
00:03:52.520 --> 00:04:00.001
Chiptest. This computer was further developed by Mr. Hsu and IBM, and 12 years later, was re-launched as Deep

37
00:04:00.002 --> 00:04:10.267
Blue. The team behind Deep Blue designed a chess-specific processor chip that is capable of examining and evaluating two to three thousand chess positions per

38
00:04:10.268 --> 00:04:17.768
second. In 1996, Deep Blue played its first six-game match against the world chess champion, Garry

39
00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:23.799
Kasparov. Although the computer won the first game, Kasparov won the match by winning three games and drawing

40
00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:31.250
two. The chess computer Hydra is the intellectual descendant of Deep Blue and is designed to be able to beat any human

41
00:04:31.251 --> 00:04:38.362
player. In June 2005, Hydra scored a decisive victory over the world’s seventh-best player, Michael

42
00:04:38.363 --> 00:04:40.750
Adams. The Great Chess

43
00:04:40.751 --> 00:04:49.956
Automaton In the eighteenth century, chess fans around the world were also captivated by another chess-playing machine─the Great Chess

44
00:04:49.957 --> 00:04:56.884
Automaton. The Automaton Chess Player was invented in 1769 by Baron Kempelen of

45
00:04:56.885 --> 00:05:05.047
Hungary. It consisted of a wooden figure dressed in Turkish clothes whose upper body was attached to a wooden box filled with gears and

46
00:05:05.048 --> 00:05:12.146
wires. The machine was wound up, and the figure would play against human opponents by moving its mechanical

47
00:05:12.147 --> 00:05:17.534
arms. The Automaton Chess Player was an excellent player and won almost every

48
00:05:17.535 --> 00:05:24.376
match. It toured Europe and America and even beat Benjamin Franklin, the brilliant American writer and

49
00:05:24.377 --> 00:05:30.032
scientist! Before each show, the machine was opened to show that a real person was not hidden

50
00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:33.080
inside. Still, most people were

51
00:05:33.081 --> 00:05:46.135
skeptical. The secret was revealed seventy years after its invention: the cleverly constructed box could, in fact, hide a full-size man (not a dwarf, as some people thought), who was a chess champion.

52
00:05:46.136 --> 00:05:48.064
Chess Around the World

53
00:05:48.065 --> 00:05:57.132
There are many variants of chess around the world, with the most popular being xiangqi in China, shogi in Japan, and janggi in Korea.

54
00:05:57.133 --> 00:06:01.939
Janggi is played on a board that is nine lines wide by ten lines long.

55
00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:08.017
Each player has 16 pieces, green and red, and their names are written in Chinese characters.

56
00:06:08.018 --> 00:06:15.056
Some character names are similar to the names used in ancient Persian chess; for example, the elephant and the chariot.

57
00:06:15.057 --> 00:06:24.539
Unlike in Western chess, where the game is concentrated in the few middle rows for most of the game, in janggi the game takes place all over the board.

58
00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:31.213
Janggi is also slower than Western chess, with professional games often lasting over one hundred moves.

59
00:06:31.214 --> 00:06:39.979
Chess has featured in popular culture, literature, customs, politics, science, technology, and even religion throughout its long history.

60
00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:43.317
There are some interesting trivia connected to this game.

61
00:06:43.318 --> 00:06:49.683
The first chess game played by telephone was played by two gentlemen in England, in 1878.

62
00:06:49.684 --> 00:07:01.464
In 1922, the world chess champion, Jose Capablanca, played 103 opponents at the same time in Cleveland, U.S.A., and drew one game and won all the rest.

63
00:07:01.465 --> 00:07:07.884
And the first chess match played between a player in space and one on Earth took place in 1970.

64
00:07:07.885 --> 00:07:15.171
Astronauts on a Russian spacecraft played their ground crew on a chess set designed specifically for the weightless environment.

65
00:07:15.172 --> 00:07:18.996
The game of chess has spread across all of the world’s oceans.

66
00:07:18.997 --> 00:07:24.996
In fact, it has been said, "There are more adventures on a chessboard than on all the seas of the world."

